hi today I wanted to talk about  something that I think is very important  and it is about how to learn the hardest  languages in the world  have you ever asked yourself and told  yourself that you didn't want to learn  that language because you heard it was  too difficult you know maybe you want to  learn Hungarian and you heard it's  impossible to learn or Mandarin Chinese  or polish I happened to meet quite a few  times for example 10 years ago a little  bit more than 10 years ago I asked my  aunt what she thought about Mandarin  Chinese she's she also the language  lover and she told me it's impossible to  learn so challenging all these  characters and tones you know it's  impossible just you know don't even  start and actually kept me from learning  Mandarin Chinese for years and years and  the same thing happened for example for  Polish or for Hungarian now I think that  what I did wrong is to believe in what  people said and and they themselves  don't even that was based on their  perception of difficulty in my aunt that  I didn't even know Mandarin Chinese so I  think that in this regard we shouldn't  talk about difficulty because difficulty  is something that is subjective all  languages can be easy all languages can  be difficult depending on who talks for  example I saw on one of the comments in  YouTube that somebody said the Japanese  is so easy but for me it's like the most  the most challenging language I've ever  heard so far so everything is relative  and subjective I think it more objective  term is complexity because languages are  complex systems like an airplane is a  complex system in the sense that it's  made of smaller and simpler parts the  same goes for a car and the language is  exactly the same it's made of many sub  parts now all languages are related in a  way in the sense that they're all made  of sub parts like phonetics grammar etc  etc and the thing is that we shouldn't  talk about difficulty and we shouldn't  talk about complexity because once again  we manage complex systems every single  day the mere fact that I'm speaking is a  complex procedure per se the fact that  we're driving  Carr was swimming we're walking they're  all complex things that we do naturally  a language learning is no exception  so I would rather talk about language or  linguistic distance between languages in  the sense that languages can be closer  or it can be not so close or distant  depending on how much the sub parts of  the languages overlap let me give an  example Italian and Spanish are close  languages or are considered close  languages because many of their  respective sub parts overlap for example  Italian Spanish overlap in terms of  pronunciation in terms of grammar  because we form sentences in a very  similar way in a very similar order and  in culture but Italian and Japanese are  quite distant because even though you  know Italian and Japanese are easier or  close in terms of pronunciation because  they don't have that many sounds and  they share quite a few sounds the fact  is that in the end their overall their  distant because the cup because of the  culture because of the way they form  sentences so it's important to talk in  terms of distance and I'm going to base  my next what what I'm going to say next  and my tips so to say based on this  concept of distance so let me give an  example it's very important when you  start when you decide to learn a  language distant language so to say to  know where you're going in this regard  what you have to do is it's paramount is  to check your assumptions when people  say this is impossible to learn remember  this is their perception of difficulty  this is what they think something that  is difficult for them might not be  difficult for you so there's a bunch of  things in which we believe that are  actually not true but that we heard so  many times that we take for granted and  getting rid of that and listen to your  inner voice is the very first step  towards this journey then the second the  second very important step is to  actually figure out why you're learning  a language it is absolutely paramount  that you understand why you're doing  something and you don't have to base it  on hearsay something like a  cool to learn Spanish so let me give it  a try or I heard that it's cool to speak  this language and you want to speak it  because it's cool in general you know or  that a lot of people are learning it  it's fashionable these are all extrinsic  reasons they come from outside they're  weak and what is going to happen that in  the long either in the short or long  term probably more in the short term  you're going to give up because you're  not supported by valid reasons the  reasons have to come from inside and you  have to think about them so that you  know they drive there they're going to  be your driving force something that  comes from inside is the result of  feelings that you develop when you when  you have certain experiences so if you  can go to the country or get to know a  native speaker spend time with the  culture of the language read a book get  interested in it that is what is going  to you know to push you forward in the  long term it's very very important even  before you start this journey this is  absolutely paramount tip number three or  step number three is the fact that you  have to plan your time so planning your  time first comes from deciding having  very clear short as long as long-term  goals meaning that's suppose that for  example a native English speaker you  could be you I want to learn German then  let's say that you want to reach in two  years you want to speak the language  fluently but then you have to think when  once you have a long term goal you have  to think about a short term goal one is  your short term goal in for example six  months what do you want to do with the  language in six months let's suppose  that you want to reach basic fluency in  six months well that means that you have  a certain amount of time six months 180  days to reach basic fluency in the  language so let's do the math 180 days  and let's say that you needs you need  moral more or less 2200 hours to reach  fluency in say basic fluency in German  so it's more or less one hour a day so  this the fact that you have a short term  goal just defines how much time you can  spend daily once you know that you can  start planning say a month on Sunday for  example you can tell yourself you know  what  I want to dedicate time to this because  time it's not something you have or  don't have something time is something  that you dedicate you find the time to  do something and you start planning the  week before and he start saying okay I'm  going to dedicate this amount of time  every day this slot this time slot on  Monday this time slot on Tuesday and so  on this is another very important thing  because if you don't plan then you know  on a day you say okay I've done a number  of things and let's let me do some  German and then you're tired and your  energy is slow and you're not going to  do that and the fourth important thing  is the how how you're going there  meaning you have to have a method so  that method is your means of transport  it could be an airplane you know it can  become an airplane if you know exactly  what to do and in terms of method it is  very important that your method is  effective for you there is no one best  method in language learning  there's just the method or the methods  that work for you and to find it is a  matter of trial and error so if somebody  says try this if you heard that it's  cool you try give it a try if you don't  like it just take a step back and try  something else until you find the thing  that you like to do this is absolutely  important and the method has to be  enjoyable you have to do things that you  like your independent language learner  nobody forces you to do things that you  don't want to do so make sure that you  enjoy every single thing that you do and  the third thing is that the method has  to be flexible in that in the case of  distant languages focus on the things  that are the most challenging at first  because then things are going to get  easier much faster down the line easier  and faster down the line meaning for  example in Chinese and Mandarin Chinese  what I did is I focused I made sure that  I focused on tones from the very  beginning because that was the most  challenging part of the language and  then things got easier down the line  because of this decision and the fourth  and in the and this is very important  and it's important that the method be  flexible once again so you have to know  to recap you have to know you have to  get rid of assumptions you have to check  your assumptions and get rid of rumors  then you have to know why you want to  learn the language then you have to  how to learn the language and then you  just it's just a matter of covering the  distance you know it's not about  difficulty anymore it's not about time  it's not about distance it's about your  will to cover this path so thanks for  listening and if you want you can check  the article that it's quite lengthy the  article that I wrote on my blog and if  you subscribe to the newsletter you've  got to have a bunch of things that you  cannot see on on YouTube and they're all  for free and sent to you and after  burying a bunch of interesting things so  once again thanks for listening and the  last question for you is how far will  you go in terms of language distance by
 

